WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.930
CASSIE: Hello and welcome.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.730
We're here at the security
zone at Next with Christiaan,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.734
an expert in phishing
attacks at Google Cloud.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:15.650
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Hi
Cassie, happy to be here.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.774
CASSIE: So phishing attacks
are a difficult problem

00:00:17.774 --> 00:00:19.430
for security teams to solve.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.310
And one of the most common ways
that phishing attackers get

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.062
access to sensitive data is
by stealing passwords, right?

00:00:26.062 --> 00:00:27.020
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Yeah.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.630
Phishing attacks are getting
more sophisticated every day.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.190
And attackers are
finding new ways

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.670
to trick employees into
accidentally handing over

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.880
their login information.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.542
CASSIE: So if I'm
a security admin

00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:38.000
and I'm trying to
protect employees

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.570
from having their passwords
stolen, where do I even start?

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.390
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Let's walk
through an example here.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.120
A phishing attacker starts
by setting up a fake website.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.890
And that website
will look exactly

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.600
like the real login
page or the service

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.350
that you're trying to log into.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.170
So the attacker would
then send a link

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.979
to this phishing
page to the employee.

00:00:55.979 --> 00:00:57.770
And that employee then
types their username

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.100
and their password into
that phishing website.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.129
CASSIE: So the employee has
just typed their username

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.977
and password into a
completely fake phishing site?

00:01:06.977 --> 00:01:08.060
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Exactly.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.770
And-- but then happens
is the attacker

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.720
opens up the real
website and they then

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.790
replay this username
and password

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.860
into that legitimate website.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.380
What's, furthermore, is the
legitimate websites then

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.920
will send a real two factor
authentication prompt or a code

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.410
to the user's device.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.155
The user, unknowingly,
accepts the prompt or types

00:01:26.155 --> 00:01:29.169
their OTP, one time password,
code into the attacker's site

00:01:29.169 --> 00:01:30.710
and, now, the
attacker, not only have

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.510
your username and your
password, they even

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.150
have your 2FA code that
will give them access

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.930
to all of your systems.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.410
With single sign on
being so widespread,

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.567
once the attacker has
access to one system,

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.650
it makes it really trivial
for them to gain access

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.080
to all other systems
with any sensitive data

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.709
that the employee
then has access to.

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:50.250
CASSIE: So the employee thinks
that they just securely logged

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.822
into a legitimate site
where, in actual fact,

00:01:52.822 --> 00:01:54.280
their account just
got compromised?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:54.840
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Exactly.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.190
Phishing attacks like
these are getting

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.130
much more sophisticated
at tricking users

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.030
into giving up their
sensitive login information.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.460
This is really a huge
problem for companies today.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.680
A recent data breach
report by Verizon

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.860
estimated that 81% of
account compromises

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.990
last year were due
to vulnerabilities

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.880
associated with passwords.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.390
CASSIE: Wow.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.100
That's a lot.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.050
So how can companies
protect employees

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.240
from falling for these
sophisticated stolen

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.274
password and 2FA attacks?

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:22.690
CHRISTIAAN BRAND:
So companies can

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.030
protect employees against
these types of attacks

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.300
by providing all employees
with FIDO security keys

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.402
that connect to their devices.

00:02:30.402 --> 00:02:32.360
Google just launched its
own FIDO certified key

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.240
called the Titan Security Key.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.240
It provides a phishing-resistant
second factor of authentication

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.070
that prevents attackers from
gaining access to systems,

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.220
even if the attacker has
stolen the employee's password.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.492
CASSIE: So how do these
security keys work?

00:02:45.492 --> 00:02:47.200
CHRISTIAAN BRAND:
Well, let's take a look

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.590
at our employee again.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:51.040
So this time, with the
Titan Security Key,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.637
when they open up
a login page, they

00:02:52.637 --> 00:02:54.220
will be prompted to
touch the security

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.990
key connected to their device.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.700
When they touch
the key, not only

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.160
is an encrypted code sent
to Google servers, which

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.620
validate that the user has
access to their Titan Security

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.750
Key, but it also
ensures that the user

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.120
is on a legitimate
Google login page.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.530
Really neat.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.770
CASSIE: So even if the attacker
steals the employee's password

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.990
and then tries to
relay their login,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.790
they still can't get
access because they

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.550
have no way of
physically authenticating

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.860
without the employee's
security key.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.000
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Exactly.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.300
Now, let's try security keys
out to show you how they work.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.030
So on the first
laptop, let's look

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.160
at a user trying to sign in.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.310
So this user is clearly
on some phishing website

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.200
and let's see how the
security key is going

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.120
to protect this user,
even though they're

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.800
in the midst of falling
for a phishing attack.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.800
So can I have that
security key for a moment?

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.820
Thank you so much.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.040
So this user will go ahead
insert their security key,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.430
try to enter their password
into this phishing website,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.460
and, at this point, when
she's trying to log in,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.320
the security key is
going to stop her

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.550
from signing in because clearly
she's on a phishing website.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.360
And even though she
didn't realize it,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.881
the security key did and
stopped her from signing in.

00:04:04.881 --> 00:04:05.380
CASSIE: OK.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.351
So this is actually going
to work on the real site

00:04:08.351 --> 00:04:10.100
or is it just going
to block both of them?

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.974
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: So
let's take a quick look.

00:04:11.974 --> 00:04:14.610
So on this laptop, Cheryl is on
the right Google sign in page,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.110
right?

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.978
If we investigate that URL up
top, everything looks good.

00:04:17.978 --> 00:04:19.269
So I'm going to go ahead, I'm--

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:20.230
CASSIE: You're
going to need this.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:21.130
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: Oh,
thank you so much.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.504
So, I'm going to
go ahead and I'm

00:04:22.504 --> 00:04:24.680
going to enter
Cheryl's password here,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.620
but before I do, of
course, I, first,

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.160
need to enter the security key.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.330
I'm going to go ahead,
I'm going to click,

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.460
and, now, you'll see how
simple this really is.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.520
All she has to do is touch
the button on the security key

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.160
and she will be magically
signed in with the security

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.250
key protecting her against
potential phishing.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.000
CASSIE: Fantastic.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.560
I love how simple that is.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.130
And it's a whole new
level of protection

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.172
against phishing attacks.

00:04:49.172 --> 00:04:50.546
CHRISTIAAN BRAND:
Exactly, right?

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:53.290
So, Titan Security Keys work
hand-in-hand with Google

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.180
and the Chrome browser
to provide employees

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.290
with end-to-end
phishing protection.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.200
In addition, Titan
Security Keys include

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.570
firmware, developed by Google,
to verify the key's integrity.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.619
They're built on
open FIDO standards

00:05:04.619 --> 00:05:06.160
and that allows
these keys to be used

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.250
with lots of other services,
including Salesforce, Facebook,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.490
and more.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.210
CASSIE: So how can someone get
started with security keys?

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.410
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: So
Titan Security Keys

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.420
will soon be available for
sale on the Google Store.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.130
And if you're a
Google Cloud customer,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.900
you can also get
these immediately

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.640
through your account executive.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.949
You can also use any
compatible FIDO security key

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:25.740
from any other vendor
with Google products.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.580
And once you have your keys, you
just need to G.co/SecurityKey

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.129
for further instructions.

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:34.420
CASSIE: Well, Christiaan,
thanks so much for walking us

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.922
through how the stolen
password attacks work

00:05:36.922 --> 00:05:38.880
and how we can protect
against them with Google

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.430
Cloud and Titan Security Keys.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:41.680
CHRISTIAAN BRAND: My pleasure.

